WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.080
- Somewhere in there about that.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.190
- How many outlets do you need?

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.183
- Uh, just two.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.230
- All right. Charlie ... Charlie Jones.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.970
Charlie, you were the
Warden at, uh, Holman Prison

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.330
in Atmore, Alabama?

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:26.750
- Right.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.080
- How many years were
you the Warden at Holman?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.590
- 30 ... oh, well, I was Warden for 18.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.430
I worked in the prison system for 35.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.210
- 35 years. And you grew
up where? In Alabama?

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.043
- Right here.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:40.850
- Tell me about your childhood.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:41.980
You had both of your parents?

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.330
- Yeah.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.520
- How would you describe your childhood?

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.980
- Ah, just normal. I went
to school, finished school,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.723
went to college, finished
that, uh, come back here,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.753
built a house,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.970
and uh, I'm nine miles from the prison.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.893
I lived here most of the time.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.640
I've been moved around
all over the state at

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.120
different prisons.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.150
- How many prisoners
did you have at Holman?

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.703
- Approximately 1,000.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.150
- And you had death row inmates at Holman?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.590
- Right.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.310
- Holman's the only prison
in Alabama that has the ...

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:22.143
- Right.

00:01:22.143 --> 00:01:23.740
- ... death penalty ... has executions ...

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.573
- Right.

00:01:24.573 --> 00:01:25.406
- Right?

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.980
- As the Warden for 18
years, what's your view on

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.300
the death penalty? Do you
see it as an effective

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.133
way to

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.710
control crime

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.683
or keep ... keep ...

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.800
keep society in line?

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.210
- Well, I think you have
to on .. on, uh, bad cases,

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.130
uh,

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.640
you can't ... you know, certain things,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.640
you can do other things, but
the way death row was set up,

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.363
uh,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.130
it's pretty bad. You have
to have a pretty bad crime

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.770
to be put on death row.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:00.680
You had to have a

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.580
combination of murder robbery,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.240
murder rape, or something
like that before you

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.190
qualify for it.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.023
But,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.680
sooner or later, these other

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.330
non-serious crimes,

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.960
in my view, have to be done
other ways because the ...

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.810
the tax-payers are not
going to pay to have all

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:26.843
of them locked up.

00:02:28.303 --> 00:02:29.136
There too many of them.

00:02:29.136 --> 00:02:30.190
- It gets expensive. Right?

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:31.023
- Yeah.

00:02:31.023 --> 00:02:34.962
- So as Warden at Holman, part
of your job was to perform

00:02:34.962 --> 00:02:37.200
the executions. Right?

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.033
- Right.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.033
- You were using the electric
chair or lethal injections?

00:02:43.003 --> 00:02:43.953
- Is was the chair.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.513
- So by the time a man
gets to the electric chair,

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.970
where do you see your role
as the end of his life?

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.500
Do you see it as you're
responsible for that?

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.353
You're the one that's pulling the trigger?

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.980
- Well, technically I guess I was, but

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.760
a lot of things happen
between the time that he's

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.023
arrested and the time he
has to sit in that chair.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.600
It's a long, drawn-out process
that can last as long as

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.630
20 years; sometimes longer than that.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.630
- There's a lot of steps
a suspect would have to

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.090
go through before he sits
in the electric chair.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:24.923
Right?

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:28.480
- Yeah. It ... a crime committed,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.170
and there's an investigation,

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.921
and then they follow the
investigation until they

00:03:34.921 --> 00:03:36.943
arrest somebody.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.870
And then he's arraigned,
and then he's tried,

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:45.340
and 12 people sit in judgment on him,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.200
and if he's convicted,
he goes to prison ...

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.593
goes to death row.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.240
Then his appeal process starts,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.693
and it is long and drawn out.

00:03:58.453 --> 00:04:01.103
The Alabama Supreme Court,
Court of Criminal Appeals,

00:04:03.508 --> 00:04:06.553
State Court. It can last on and on.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.050
- So by the time he gets
to the electric chair,

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.640
it's pretty certain that this
is ... that he is guilty?

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.400
- By the time ... yep. Well, not always.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.640
There's some time they can
go right up pretty close

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.750
to the time, and the attorney
come up with something

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.290
that he appealed one last time to the

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.910
Alabama Supreme Court,

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.920
and they'll give him a stay.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.993
It don't always last.

00:04:37.371 --> 00:04:40.634
It might last a day. It might last a week.

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.334
It might last three or four years.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.273
But most of the time,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.330
once I'm served ... or was served

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.323
with a warrant ... a death warrant,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.783
then it's getting really serious.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.963
I'd get that warrant 30
days prior to the date,

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.860
and during that time, the
attorneys for both sides ...

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.500
they get busy,

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.470
and they try to come up with

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.140
something to stop it.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.980
Sometimes they do.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:17.813
And

00:05:17.813 --> 00:05:19.923
by that time, most times, they didn't.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.370
- What does that death row
inmate's last moments like

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:25.370
before ... before he

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:27.783
is executed?

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.410
Can you describe that?

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.740
- Well, you're talking about the day-of?

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:33.573
- The day of. Yeah.

00:05:33.573 --> 00:05:36.040
- Well, the day of, he's ...

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.190
the inmate would spend

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:41.290
most of the day with his
family on the visiting yard.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:43.960
We'd leave him out there
approximately two hours

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.490
before the

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.640
time to get him ready.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.120
And we'd take him off the
visiting yard, escort him

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.203
back to his cell.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.200
The execution team would get him dressed,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.583
shave his head, shave
his ankle ... left ankle,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.130
and get him ready. By
that time, it's right

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.913
around 12 ...

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.810
12 a.m.; 12:01 a.m.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.780
And they ...

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.460
they would call me. I
would be in my office.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.010
I stayed in my office ...

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.093
- 12 a.m. Midnight?

00:06:21.221 --> 00:06:22.054
- Do what?

00:06:22.054 --> 00:06:22.887
- Midnight?

00:06:22.887 --> 00:06:23.720
- Yeah.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.553
- Yeah.

00:06:24.553 --> 00:06:27.240
I'd stay in my office
while they did that in case

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.060
we got a call from the
governor, or from the

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.923
Attorney General, or from
the Court, putting it off.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.920
If we didn't, then somebody
would man the phone while I

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.490
would go to the death chamber.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.060
By the time I got down
there, they've got him in

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.600
the chair,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.763
strapped in, ready to go.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:54.473
By Law, I had to read
that death warrant to him.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.107
And I would ask him if he
had any last request or ...

00:07:00.107 --> 00:07:01.513
... or statement.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.733
Most of them didn't.

00:07:05.448 --> 00:07:06.930
If not,

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.190
then I would tell the two officers in the

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.133
death chamber to make him ready.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.690
Final ... final

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.080
readiness was putting the helmet on him

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.033
and putting the ankle bracelet on him.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.417
And then, I would leave the death chamber,

00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:29.060
and go in next door to the equipment room.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:30.973
The two officers would come out.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.730
We didn't want to leave
anybody in there with him

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:38.783
for fear that somebody might
pass out and fall into him.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.123
And once they

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:46.050
come out, they'd give me a signal:

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.373
Tap on the door.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:49.483
And

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:51.830
it was all over with.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.100
- It's just a flip of a switch?

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.360
- Press of a button.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.360
- Press of a button?

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.120
How did this effect you
personally, doing this?

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:02.953
- Well ...

00:08:02.953 --> 00:08:04.280
- How many did you perform?

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:08.210
- I did 17 men and one woman.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.880
- How did if effect you personally?

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:10.713
- Well,

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.713
that's a good question.

00:08:14.713 --> 00:08:18.370
I've never let their problem

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.670
become my problem.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.340
And I didn't put them
there. I didn't go out

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.390
and arrest them, and
sit in judgment on them.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:29.493
Once they got there,
that was their problem.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.910
And

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:32.863
they knew that.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.460
And I tried to treat them in

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.300
such a way, and my staff
treat them in such a way

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:44.370
that when the time came,
you know, we didn't have

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.929
to look away from them. We
could look them in the eye,

00:08:47.929 --> 00:08:50.940
and let them know what's
fixing to take place,

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.587
and what to watch for, and
you know, "Here it comes."

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:57.773
And afterwards,

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:01.240
you know, it lays on your mind.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:02.900
It did me.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.163
But not a guilt thing.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.690
Just hoping that I had done everything

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:13.290
to be sure

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:18.100
that his case was as
perfect as it could be,

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:23.100
that there was no way me or
my staff the cause of him

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:28.740
having to sit in that chair
when maybe he shouldn't have.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.790
If he shouldn't have, then
it was somebody else's

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.120
mishandling; not us.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.070
So, you know, I ...

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:39.453
I don't lose sleep over it.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:43.790
To tell you the truth,

00:09:45.515 --> 00:09:47.103
I very seldom think about it.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.120
- What kind of reactions
would you see from the

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.410
men as they approach their last day?

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.490
- Not ... not a lot.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:58.617
Most of them

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:00.923
was ready.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:05.170
They would ... you know, we
would take them out of their

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:09.300
regular cell 24 hours ahead
of time and put them in

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.273
what we call the Ready
Cell, the Death Cell.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:16.183
Once we got them down there,

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:18.880
and they were away from all of their

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.943
friends on the cell block,

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.500
they settled down really well.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.390
I can only recall one

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.400
that seemed like he ...

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.300
he carried a grudge to the chair,

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:36.860
but the rest of them didn't.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.053
They let it go,

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:41.063
and

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.230
most of them, at one time or another,

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:49.133
during that short period,
admitted what they had done.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.090
- Eventually they admitted their crimes?

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:53.623
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:56.450
Not all of them, but most of them.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.280
- Is there one in
particular that stood out

00:10:58.280 --> 00:10:59.113
in your memory?

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:02.533
- Yeah. But I can't recall his name.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.303
He ...

00:11:09.422 --> 00:11:10.640
you've seen people that

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.890
carried a ... I don't
know what you'd call it,

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:19.399
but they were mad all of the time.

00:11:19.399 --> 00:11:21.773
You had to be careful
how you spoke to them.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:26.390
You'd say, "Good morning," to
them, they'd get mad at you.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.190
That's kind of the way this guy was.

00:11:30.808 --> 00:11:32.947
- What do you think the core cause is of a

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.390
hardened criminal's

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.560
behavior? I mean, do you think

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.705
it was his environment? Or his family?

00:11:41.705 --> 00:11:43.290
Or his brain chemistry?

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:47.223
What do you think is the cause
of what makes a criminal?

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.110
- I don't know. If I knew
that, I could probably rest

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:54.500
easy the rest of my life.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.420
I don't know.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.533
I think it's a combination of things.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:03.583
I think a lot of them
grew up without a father,

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.573
not knowing who their father was,

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:12.123
and getting with the wrong
crowd as they grew up,

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.933
taking ... making bad choices,

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:18.103
and

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.090
going around, trying
to take away stuff that

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:23.353
didn't belong to them.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.430
- It all goes hand-in-hand. Doesn't it?

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:28.270
- Do what?

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:29.643
- It goes hand-in-hand.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.710
- It's ... you know, to
answer your question,

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.720
I think it's ... it's just
... it's a combination

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:37.600
of all that stuff.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:41.430
- You're retired now,
but in your decades of

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:42.981
working in the prison system,

00:12:42.981 --> 00:12:45.740
how have you seen criminals change?

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.610
I mean, did you see they're more violent?

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.507
How are they different now
than they were in the past?

00:12:51.507 --> 00:12:52.340
- Uh ...

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.580
- Drugs are a bigger part
of the picture now. Right?

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:55.413
- The what?

00:12:55.413 --> 00:12:56.680
- Drugs are a bigger part
of the picture now. Correct?

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.900
- Oh, yeah. Uh-huh.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:03.900
Most of ... not on death row,
but regular population ...

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.340
most of the inmates in the
regular population that

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:12.650
it maybe didn't come to
prison because of drugs;

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:14.913
but drugs was always involved.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.770
- How do you look at this
part of your life that you ...

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.150
that you had? I mean,
this chapter of your life?

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:25.380
It's certainly very unusual.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:26.940
How do you view it?

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.050
- Well, I guess I look at it like

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:33.733
anybody else looks at
something that they do.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:35.640
A farmer,

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.473
or

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.000
whatever.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.780
It's something that I did,
and I knew what it was

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:46.000
when I took the the job.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.350
It wasn't no surprise. You know,

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:50.570
I knew that eventually

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.630
that I'd have to do that.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.363
And now, you know, I look back on it,

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:57.043
and

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.540
I know I did everything I could

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:02.333
to see that they were treated
... not just at death row,

00:14:02.333 --> 00:14:07.333
but all of them,

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:11.420
they were treated right, wasn't abused,

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:15.500
and at the same time, now, we didn't ...

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.863
we didn't let them us
do us that way either.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:20.853
But we didn't have that much trouble.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:24.160
And I look back on that

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:24.993
as that I

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.340
did all I could to be sure they ...

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:30.500
they were treated right.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:33.093
I've met them on the street,

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:38.820
and they would run to me with a hand out;

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:41.343
and you know, some of them I'd recognize,

00:14:42.653 --> 00:14:45.210
some of them I didn't. And
they had to tell me who

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:46.043
they were.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:50.820
I've had some of them tell
me, you know, that I'd

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.633
kept them from getting
into bad trouble in prison,

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:56.723
and

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:03.040
I knew by something I
could gauge what I did,

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.800
maybe ... maybe I did

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.210
all I could to

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:09.483
do what was right.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:16.190
I don't ... I don't have
any regrets of anything.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.777
And the main thing, I
mentioned it a while ago,

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:24.310
I did not let people
put me on a guilt trip

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:26.250
about what I had to do,

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:31.130
and I did not allow the inmate's problem

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.803
to become my problem.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.893
They had to do what they had to do,

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:40.337
I did what I had to do,

00:15:40.337 --> 00:15:43.273
and they just have to
let the rough end drag.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:46.963
- All right, Charlie.

00:15:46.963 --> 00:15:49.420
Thank you so much for talking with me.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.143
Very interesting. Thank you.

